# A waymarker on a grass shoulder below the pass, late in the afternoon.
#
#     loom render assets/test/mountain_pass.loom --out pass.png --size 1600x1000
#
# **The first landscape in the library built out of one `heightfield` op**, and
# the first that puts the imported props at their real size against something
# big enough to make that size mean something. `props.loom` proves the models
# import; this is the scene that asks whether a 3 m log and a 0.48 m lantern
# still read as a log and a lantern when the thing behind them is 60 m tall.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The eye stands at 1.8 m on the lip of a turf shoulder and looks east-north-east
# up the basin. Four things carry the frame and each is doing one job:
#
# - **The waypost**, dead centre on the crest, silhouetted against the col
#   between the two summits. It is the only vertical in the picture and the only
#   straight line; a 2.1 m mast with a 0.96 m lantern on it is the number the
#   eye reads the mountains against. *Broken:* move it a couple of metres either
#   way along Z and it stops being against sky and starts being against rock,
#   at which point a dark iron fixture on a dark slope is invisible — which is
#   what the first three renders of it were.
# - **The log and the stump**, five to eight metres out on the left. Human scale
#   at arm's length, where the grass is drawn blade by blade, so there is a
#   size reference at *both* ends of the depth range rather than only at the far
#   one.
# - **The bedrock shelf**, right of centre, half buried. See its node for why
#   half.
# - **The peaks**, 60 to 95 m away, and they are only peaks because of the fog.
#   `fog_density = 0.011` is what separates the summit group from the ridge
#   behind it; at the 0.002 most scenes here use, the whole massif reads as one
#   grey cut-out and the scene is the grey lump this kind of scene always
#   risks being.
#
# **Yaw 278, and it was rendered rather than reasoned about.** Almost every
# scene in this library is yaw 0, so the convention was untested; four renders
# from one point at 0/90/180/270 settle it — **yaw 270 looks down +X**, and
# screen-right is then +Z. Everything in the layout below follows from that,
# so if the convention ever changes this file's composition is the first
# casualty and re-deriving it is four renders.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The terrain is one op, and the three numbers in it are the whole landform
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `VoxelOp::Heightfield`'s slope is bounded by
# `amplitude * 3 * octaves * frequency / norm` (that is `lipschitz()`, rearranged),
# and the *first octave's* share of it — `amplitude * 3 * frequency / norm` — is
# what decides whether the big shapes are mountains or downland. The first pass
# at this scene used `amplitude 26, frequency 0.009`: 0.36, which is 20°, and it
# rendered as moorland. **Mountains want the product `amplitude x frequency`,
# not either one alone**; 58 x 0.018 puts the first octave at 1.67 (59°) with a
# 56 m primary wavelength, and that is the landform in the frame.
#
# `octaves = 4` rather than 6 is the second lesson. Every octave contributes the
# same slope bound, so six of them do not make the mountains steeper — they only
# add detail at 1.7 m, which is under two voxels here and rendered as gravel
# crawling over the whole massif.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The voxel budget, measured, and the surprise in it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Bake cost is `chunks x 32768` voxel evaluations and nothing else, because a
# heightfield's `lipschitz()` here is 6.8 — the early-out that fills a uniform
# chunk in O(1) needs the sample point to be 6.8 chunk-radii clear of the
# surface, which almost nothing is. So the chunk count *is* the cost.
#
#     0.6 m, chunks [8, 6, 8]   384 chunks   220.7 ms/frame   <- 7x over budget
#     1.2 m, chunks [4, 2, 4]    32 chunks    15.4 ms/frame   <- shipped
#
# Both measured with `loom run … --edit --play --frames 90` on the **debug**
# build, which is what `cargo xtask validate` gates on; the release figure for
# the shipped setting is 10.7 ms, and ~99% of both numbers is the one-off bake
# amortised over 90 frames. `lanternhead` is 21.0 ms on the same instrument.
#
# **Coarsening it four times over made the mountains look better, not worse**,
# and that was not the trade anyone expected. At 0.6 m every lit slope carried
# horizontal corrugations that read as contour lines on a relief model — the
# last noise octave, sampled just above its own Nyquist. At 1.2 m that octave is
# below the voxel and simply is not there, and what is left is clean ridgelines.
# **So resolution was not buying detail here, it was buying an artifact.** The
# near ground under the props is the only place 1.2 m costs anything, and the
# props are meshes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The volume is a 76.8 m window onto a 116 m heightfield
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A heightfield is unbounded in X and Z and its op declares bounds of
# `base ± amplitude` — 116 m here — but the ground that actually exists over
# this footprint runs **27.2 m to 88.6 m**, measured at twenty-odd points. Two
# chunks of 1.2 m voxels is 76.8 m, so the volume is *offset* to sit over the
# band that has terrain in it: `Ground` carries `pos = [0, 18, 0]` and the op's
# `base` is 40 (world 58) to match. Same terrain in world space, a third of the
# chunks.
#
# **Both margins are what to check if this file is ever edited.** Terrain above
# the volume is cut off flat — a summit with a plateau on it. Terrain below it
# leaves a column with no surface at all, which is a hole you can see the sky
# through. As authored there is 6.2 m of headroom over the highest summit and
# 9.2 m under the deepest valley floor, and raising `amplitude` or lowering
# `base` spends both.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How the prop heights were found, since nothing in the CLI asks directly
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `loom terrain` is a recipe tool and takes no scene; `loom place --op` places
# against a node's *bounding box*, which for a voxel volume is its ceiling. The
# one query that answers "how high is the ground at X, Z" is
# **`loom water --at x,z`**, which reports `bed.height` — so every Y in this
# file came from a scratch copy of it carrying a `WaterBody` at
# `surface_height = 200` and nothing else. It is worth knowing before the next
# person guesses, and worth re-running if the terrain op is ever touched: a
# prop 20 cm in the air is obvious and a prop 20 cm under is gone.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not in GOLDEN, and the reason is the rule rather than the cost
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every rendering path here already has a reference: voxel terrain and the
# two-material slope blend are `ground.loom`, vertex-shader grass over a marched
# terrain is `grass_slope.loom`, bindless albedo and normal maps on mesh
# geometry are `materials.loom`. The one thing this scene has that no golden
# scene does is the **`heightfield` op**, and that is a CPU bake rather than a
# rendering path — `terrain_stress.loom` carries it in `SCENES` on exactly that
# basis. So this goes in `SCENES` for the same reason `props.loom` does: what is
# worth guarding is that it still loads, validates and stays inside the CPU
# budget, not its pixels.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "0c9f5b2e-6a41-4d38-9e07-3b18c4d7a521"

[[asset]]
key = "grass_ground_albedo"
id = "247fe03c-9304-4079-9a10-8159cea7db83"
path = "../textures/grass_ground_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "grass_ground_normal"
id = "08873186-0a37-46a1-8fdc-8669cf03f6fb"
path = "../textures/grass_ground_normal.png"

[[asset]]
key = "ground_rock_albedo"
id = "3d90f6b1-27ca-4e58-b7d4-5081a92fe6c7"
path = "../textures/ground_rock_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "ground_rock_normal"
id = "5e14b8c3-90af-4d26-a371-6c48d0e173b9"
path = "../textures/ground_rock_normal.png"

[[asset]]
key = "mesh_RockBeach"
id = "3774fb7f-f3ff-4cca-ae14-670f84582b8f"
path = "../meshes/rock_beach.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "rock_beach_albedo"
id = "102bd405-b85b-48a2-8211-6543cc05e6d1"
path = "../textures/rock_beach_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "rock_beach_normal"
id = "96503df9-869e-4964-ba97-22d554d66a89"
path = "../textures/rock_beach_normal.png"

[[asset]]
key = "mesh_RockBoulderA"
id = "299ddd04-f7a4-4d71-9d20-2ec12f9d06d9"
path = "../meshes/rock_boulder_a.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "rock_boulder_a_albedo"
id = "491e578b-5c25-4bbf-9875-d9a0a8456842"
path = "../textures/rock_boulder_a_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "rock_boulder_a_normal"
id = "712a499b-8aa8-4794-8713-e9cadf69f9e1"
path = "../textures/rock_boulder_a_normal.png"

# **`rock_boulder_b` has no atlas in `assets/textures/`**, and this scene does
# not lend it boulder A's. Every one of these maps is baked for one model's UV
# layout, so A's atlas on B's UVs smears A's photograph across B's faces —
# which is a mistake already in this repo's history. The mossy boulder below
# therefore carries a flat albedo and no maps, and reads as an untextured rock
# at mid distance, which is the honest version of not having the file.
[[asset]]
key = "mesh_RockBoulderB"
id = "054f439d-098e-4567-a6d8-721b841b7225"
path = "../meshes/rock_boulder_b.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "mesh_TreeLog"
id = "99f25dc6-1b13-4a6d-912d-bf7eed46f35f"
path = "../meshes/tree_log.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "tree_log_albedo"
id = "7e0b0ed4-d1c0-4dfa-b29a-a01acc95aed7"
path = "../textures/tree_log_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "tree_log_normal"
id = "24a58bae-5498-42b0-8a57-d3d149876393"
path = "../textures/tree_log_normal.png"

[[asset]]
key = "mesh_TreeStump"
id = "2f64518b-4353-4598-acbe-eed598fa57de"
path = "../meshes/tree_stump.obj"

[[asset]]
key = "tree_stump_albedo"
id = "f1ba9218-6780-4e22-b2c2-1a8b3bdbd22c"
path = "../textures/tree_stump_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "tree_stump_normal"
id = "ae1d3b44-cc8f-4356-ada6-44a6735fe918"
path = "../textures/tree_stump_normal.png"

[[asset]]
key = "mesh_Lantern"
id = "d49c21f7-9c58-4ee3-b0a8-5b08a1ae88f2"
path = "../meshes/lantern.obj#WallLanternExterior001_Frame_Geo_LOD0"

[[asset]]
key = "lantern_albedo"
id = "a653ee1b-13cf-42e7-aa29-7c82ba34e84b"
path = "../textures/lantern_albedo.png"

[[asset]]
key = "lantern_normal"
id = "7ed14d43-b570-4d8e-a700-62c2cc63549a"
path = "../textures/lantern_normal.png"

[[node]]
name = "Pass"

  # **Sun 24.8° up and over the camera's left shoulder**, which is the only
  # arrangement that models a mountain rather than flattening it: a face turned
  # toward the eye is lit, the face beside it is not, and the ridge between them
  # is the line that says which way the rock runs. `lanternhead` and `homestead`
  # both put the sun almost down the camera's axis because both are about water,
  # and Fresnel needs the grazing angle; nothing here is wet and a backlit
  # massif is a silhouette.
  #
  # `ambient = 0.45` is high for this library and it is doing a real job.
  # Shadowed rock at albedo 0.44 under the 0.30 this scene started with came out
  # near black, so half the mountain was a hole. Alpine shade is bounced sky,
  # not absence of light.
  #
  # **`cloud_cover = 0.45` is here for the top third of the frame.** The
  # composition puts the horizon low, so a quarter of the picture is sky and an
  # empty gradient there reads as a missing background. There is no `Rain` in
  # this scene, so cover is only doing that and softening the key slightly.
  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [-0.52, 0.42, 0.75]
  sun_strength = 1.6
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.88, 0.72]
  ambient = 0.45
  sky_zenith = [0.10, 0.20, 0.42]
  sky_horizon = [0.55, 0.62, 0.72]
  cloud_cover = 0.45
  cloud_scale = 900.0
  fog_density = 0.011
  fog_falloff = 0.045

  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 200.0
  speed = 4.0
  gustiness = 1.1
  turbulence = 1.0
  ground_drag = 0.45

[[node]]
name = "Ground"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 18.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.VoxelVolume]
  voxel_size = 1.2
  chunks = [4, 2, 4]

  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "heightfield"
  base = 40.0
  amplitude = 58.0
  frequency = 0.018
  octaves = 4
  seed = 920731
  mode = "union"

  # **Turf is the BASE and rock is the LAYER, which is the opposite of
  # `lanternhead` and right for the same reason.** `groundLayerWeight` is
  # `1 - saturate((normal.y - cutoff)/fade)`, so the layer is a *slope* rule and
  # dead-flat ground is always the base material. A quay deck is flat, so
  # `lanternhead` had to make stone the base; a mountain's flats are its valley
  # floor and its turf shoulders, and its steep ground is bedrock. Author it the
  # other way round here and the shoulder the whole shot stands on becomes a
  # scree slope with grass growing on the cliffs above it.
  #
  # Green, because `meadow.loom`'s rule applies to every scene with a `Grass`
  # field: blades fade to nothing with distance and the ground is what the far
  # field reads as. `uv_scale = 0.35` against a 4096 map is 1434 texels/m, past
  # the ~400/m a near field can show. Triplanar because Surface Nets emits no
  # UVs at all.
  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.48, 0.52, 0.40]
  roughness = 1.0
  porosity = 0.7
  albedo_map = { asset = "grass_ground_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "grass_ground_normal" }
  uv_scale = [0.35, 0.35]
  triplanar = true

  # `slope = 0.62` and **both grass fields carry `slope_cutoff = 0.62`**; two
  # different thresholds on one hillside draw two concentric rings, which
  # `ground.loom` states and the schema's defaults pin.
  #
  # `uv_scale = 2.0` — one tile every half metre, 2048 texels/m. The map is a
  # cracked-slab photograph and at the 1.0 this started on it tiled at one metre
  # and read as crocodile scale on the near cliffs. The bare rock at the bottom
  # left of the frame is this layer doing its job on the lip the camera stands
  # on, not a hole in the turf field.
  [node.components.Material.layer]
  albedo = [0.44, 0.43, 0.41]
  roughness = 0.82
  porosity = 0.12
  uv_scale = [2.0, 2.0]
  slope = 0.62
  albedo_map = { asset = "ground_rock_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "ground_rock_normal" }

# Two fields rather than one big rectangle, for the reason `homestead` gives:
# `loom_grass` does not know where the shot is, and a field wide enough to reach
# the far shoulder would also grow blades on ground the camera never sees and
# pay a ground-grid march for all of it. `TurfNear` is centred four metres in
# front of the eye and reaches past it, so there is no bald patch under the
# camera; `TurfFar` is the next shoulder along, which is the one the eye reads
# the depth against.
#
# `height = 0.28` against `meadow`'s 0.40 — this is turf at 1500 m, and a 0.41 m
# log lying in 0.40 m grass is a log nobody can see.

[[node]]
name = "TurfNear"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [64.0, 0.0, 90.0] }

  [node.components.Grass]
  half_extent = [14.0, 14.0]
  slope_cutoff = 0.62
  density = 180.0
  height = 0.28
  width = 0.020
  clump_facing = 0.78
  clump_colour = 0.42

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.26, 0.36, 0.14]
  roughness = 0.8

[[node]]
name = "TurfFar"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [88.0, 0.0, 96.0] }

  [node.components.Grass]
  half_extent = [11.0, 11.0]
  slope_cutoff = 0.62
  density = 180.0
  height = 0.28
  width = 0.020
  clump_facing = 0.78
  clump_colour = 0.42

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.26, 0.36, 0.14]
  roughness = 0.8

# **Bedrock, and it is buried on purpose.** `rock_beach.obj` is a 6.2 m pile of
# slabs with real holes in it — open it in `props.loom` and there are black
# voids straight through the mesh — so standing it free on a hillside gives a
# broken lump, which is what two renders of this scene showed. Sunk so its base
# is 1.0 m under the ground at the uphill end and about 1.1 m proud at the
# downhill one, the voids are underground and what is left is a ledge of strata
# breaking out of the turf, which is exactly what this ground would do.
#
# The 25° yaw runs its long axis along the contour rather than down it. Across
# the 6.2 m span the ground falls 1.9 m; across the 3 m width it falls under a
# metre, which is inside the burial.

[[node]]
name = "Shelf"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [71.5, 73.0, 92.5], rot_euler = [0.0, 25.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_RockBeach" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
  roughness = 0.88
  albedo_map = { asset = "rock_beach_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "rock_beach_normal" }
  uv_scale = [1.0, 1.0]

[[node]]
name = "Log"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [61.8, 73.2, 88.8], rot_euler = [0.0, 15.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_TreeLog" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
  roughness = 0.82
  albedo_map = { asset = "tree_log_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "tree_log_normal" }
  uv_scale = [1.0, 1.0]

[[node]]
name = "Stump"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [60.5, 73.7, 86.8], rot_euler = [0.0, -35.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_TreeStump" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
  roughness = 0.85
  albedo_map = { asset = "tree_stump_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "tree_stump_normal" }
  uv_scale = [1.0, 1.0]

# Three erratics on the shoulder, two of them the same 1.2 m mesh at different
# yaws and one at 1.4x, which is the cheapest way to get variety out of a
# five-model set without any of them reading as a copy.

[[node]]
name = "BoulderNear"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [68.0, 74.2, 91.0], rot_euler = [0.0, 35.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_RockBoulderA" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
  roughness = 0.9
  albedo_map = { asset = "rock_boulder_a_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "rock_boulder_a_normal" }
  uv_scale = [1.0, 1.0]

[[node]]
name = "BoulderFar"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [78.0, 73.5, 93.0], rot_euler = [0.0, -60.0, 0.0], scale = [1.4, 1.4, 1.4] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_RockBoulderA" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
  roughness = 0.9
  albedo_map = { asset = "rock_boulder_a_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "rock_boulder_a_normal" }
  uv_scale = [1.0, 1.0]

[[node]]
name = "BoulderMossy"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [70.5, 69.5, 94.5], rot_euler = [0.0, 110.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_RockBoulderB" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.30, 0.33, 0.24]
  roughness = 0.92

# **The waymarker, and it deliberately has NO `Light`.**
#
# `lantern.obj` is a *wall* fixture — a bracket and a lamp with no base — so it
# needs something behind it, which is what the mast is; the two `o` groups in
# the file are picked apart with the `file.obj#Object` fragment syntax at the
# top of this file, and taking the frame alone leaves the glass out. The lantern
# is yawed -90° because the model faces +Z by default (`props.loom` mounts it
# that way) and this camera looks down +X.
#
# **A lit lamp on a 2 m post cannot be authored at this sun strength, and the
# arithmetic says so before the render does.** `Light::intensity` is `d² / albedo`
# for a surface `d` metres away. The pool on the ground 2 m below wants about 8;
# at 8 the mast 0.3 m from the lamp receives `8 * 0.19 / 0.09` — twenty times
# fully lit — and renders as a featureless cream column, which is what the first
# two renders of this scene showed. Backing off to 3 and moving the light 0.6 m
# clear still leaves the post at 1.2x white, because the ratio between 0.6 m and
# 2 m is squared and it does not care what the numbers are.
#
# So the lantern here is an unlit fixture at three in the afternoon, which is
# what a waymarker lamp is at three in the afternoon. A scene that wants this
# lamp burning has to be authored at dusk, and then it is `lanternhead`'s
# problem and `lanternhead` already solves it.

[[node]]
name = "WaypostMast"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [66.2, 75.2, 89.0], scale = [0.08, 1.05, 0.08] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.19, 0.16, 0.13]
  roughness = 0.88

[[node]]
name = "Waypost"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [65.9, 75.6, 89.0], rot_euler = [0.0, -90.0, 0.0], scale = [2.0, 2.0, 2.0] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "mesh_Lantern" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [1.0, 1.0, 1.0]
  roughness = 0.45
  albedo_map = { asset = "lantern_albedo" }
  normal_map = { asset = "lantern_normal" }
  uv_scale = [1.0, 1.0]

# The shot. The ground here is 75.58, so the eye is at 1.82 m.
#
# **Pitch -10 rather than -4, and it is the foreground that decides it.** The
# shoulder falls away from the lip the camera stands on, so at a shallow pitch
# the log and the stump slide off the bottom of the frame and a quarter of the
# picture is empty sky. Ten degrees down puts both in the lower third with the
# summits still clear of the crest, which is the only arrangement where the
# scene has a near, a middle and a far.

[[node]]
name = "Camera"
parent = "Pass"
transform = { pos = [55.0, 77.4, 90.0], rot_euler = [-10.0, 278.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.Camera]
  fov_y_degrees = 52.0
